\details{
\subsection{Bounded FIML source intervals (development)}{

Supply \code{source_fit} and \code{effect} by name and choose \code{method} explicitly. Only
accepted three-variable Gaussian random-X simple mediation fits from \code{\link[=fit_sem]{fit_sem()}}
are supported, with the direct path free or fixed exactly at zero. This route
does not support callbacks, plugin estimates, clusters or additional arguments.
Bounded FIML objects supplied as \code{mediation_data} or \code{data} remain refused.
Object-aware guards run before package-controlled RNG, workers or refits; opaque
user callbacks and external-first dispatch are outside that guarantee.

\code{method = "nonparametric"} gives a retained-row FIML percentile interval:
resample entire nonempty records at fixed retained N, preserving missingness and
duplicate rows, and refit the identical model with the accepted source protocol.
\code{method = "parametric"} gives a Gaussian parameter-draw percentile interval,
not a synthetic-data bootstrap: jointly draw the canonical free parameters using
the full observed/model covariance in original coordinates, without refitting.
The symmetric correlation-root factor permits only roundoff repairs; materially
asymmetric or indefinite covariance is refused. Fixed zeros stay exact. Negative
nuisance-variance draws are not filtered or truncated before evaluating the effect.

Both routes retain the original fitted effect as the point estimate and use
type-7 empirical quantiles. Every requested replicate is recorded in \verb{@inference}
(schema \code{fiml-bootstrap-1}), including indices or draw IDs, reasons, source/RNG
provenance and retained/collected counts. Any failed, invalid or nonfinite required
replicate withholds the entire interval. Interruption returns an incomplete
length-\code{n_boot} result with interrupted/unattempted IDs and \code{NA} unresolved values.
Print, summary, tidy and changed-level \code{confint()} cannot report a survivor interval;
tidy distribution SD is also unavailable for withheld results. An all-success SD
is descriptive, not a robust standard error.

The target is conditional on retained nonempty records. Assumptions include IID
jointly Gaussian data, ignorable MAR with distinct missingness/model parameters,
positivity and identified information. Retention normalization must be independent
of model parameters; independently empty MCAR records suffice. Arbitrary
value-dependent empty-row deletion and MNAR are not covered. Numerical verification
does not establish interval coverage; statistical admission remains deferred.
See the Methods and Formulas article, Bounded FIML, for the mathematical definition.
These source intervals are development functionality added after 0.16.0.
}

\subsection{Bootstrap Methods}{

\strong{Parametric Bootstrap} (\code{method = "parametric"}):
\itemize{
\item Samples parameter vectors from \eqn{N(\hat{\theta}, \hat{\Sigma})}{N(theta-hat, Sigma-hat)}
\item Fast and efficient
\item Assumes asymptotic normality of parameters
\item Recommended for most applications with n > 50
\item Requires \code{mediation_data} argument
}

\strong{Nonparametric Bootstrap} (\code{method = "nonparametric"}):
\itemize{
\item Resamples observations with replacement
\item Refits models for each bootstrap sample
\item More robust, no normality assumption
\item Computationally intensive
\item Use when normality is questionable or n is small
\item Requires \code{data} argument
}

\strong{Plugin Estimator} (\code{method = "plugin"}):
\itemize{
\item Computes point estimate only
\item No confidence interval
\item Fastest method
\item Use for quick checks or when CI not needed
\item Requires \code{mediation_data} argument
}
}

\subsection{Statistic Function}{

The \code{statistic_fn} should be a function that:
\itemize{
\item For parametric/plugin: Takes a named numeric vector of parameters
\item For nonparametric: Takes a data frame
\item Returns a single numeric value
}

Common statistic functions for indirect effect:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{# Using parameter names from MediationData
indirect_fn <- function(theta) \{
  theta["m_X"] * theta["y_M"]
\}
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\subsection{Parallel Processing}{

Set \code{parallel = TRUE} to use multiple cores:
\itemize{
\item Uses \code{parallel::mclapply()} on Unix systems
\item Falls back to sequential on Windows
\item Automatically detects available cores
}
}

\subsection{Reproducibility}{

Always set a seed for reproducible results:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{bootstrap_mediation(..., seed = 12345)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

A fixed seed gives the same resamples for any \code{ncores}, and for sequential runs, because every
resample is drawn before the work is split across cores. This holds for the nonparametric and
cluster methods (earlier versions drew inside each worker). The statistic is still computed in the
workers, so a \code{statistic_fn} that uses random numbers itself is not covered (in a sequential run its
draws now follow all the resampling draws instead of interleaving with them, so its stream differs
from earlier versions), and floating-point results can differ in the last digits when BLAS threads
differ. The resamples are held in memory before the refits start: \code{n_boot} integer vectors of length
\code{n} (about \code{4 * n * n_boot} bytes; 800 MB for \code{n = 100000} and \code{n_boot = 2000}).
}
}
\examples{
# Fit mediation model
med_data <- fit_mediation(
  formula_y = outcome ~ treatment + mediator1 + covariate1 + covariate2,
  formula_m = mediator1 ~ treatment + covariate1 + covariate2,
  data = mediation_demo,
  treatment = "treatment",
  mediator = "mediator1"
)

# Define indirect effect function
indirect_fn <- function(theta) theta["m_treatment"] * theta["y_mediator1"]

# Plugin estimator (point estimate only, fastest)
result_plugin <- bootstrap_mediation(
  statistic_fn = indirect_fn,
  method = "plugin",
  mediation_data = med_data
)
print(result_plugin)

\donttest{
# Parametric bootstrap (recommended for most applications)
result <- bootstrap_mediation(
  statistic_fn = indirect_fn,
  method = "parametric",
  mediation_data = med_data,
  n_boot = 1000,
  ci_level = 0.95,
  seed = 12345
)
print(result)

# Nonparametric bootstrap (slower but more robust)
refit_fn <- function(boot_data) {
  fit_m <- lm(mediator1 ~ treatment + covariate1 + covariate2,
              data = boot_data)
  fit_y <- lm(outcome ~ treatment + mediator1 + covariate1 + covariate2,
              data = boot_data)
  unname(coef(fit_m)["treatment"] * coef(fit_y)["mediator1"])
}

result_np <- bootstrap_mediation(
  statistic_fn = refit_fn,
  method = "nonparametric",
  data = mediation_demo,
  n_boot = 500,
  seed = 12345
)
print(result_np)
}

